# Build a reusable component

Rocci components are ordinary Roc functions with a bounded template body. This
guide builds a status card that accepts typed data, branches on state, and ships
its styles beside the markup.

## Define the data model

Ordinary declarations outside components stay ordinary Roc:

```rocci
import Html

Status : [Ready, Working, Failed]

statusLabel = |status|
    match status {
        Ready => "Ready"
        Working => "Working"
        Failed => "Failed"
    }
```

## Add the component

```rocci
@component StatusCard = |{ title, status }, content| {
    @css {
        .status-card {
            padding: 1rem;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 0.75rem;
        }
        .status-card strong { display: block; }
    }
    <section class="status-card">
        <strong>{title}</strong>
        <span>{statusLabel(status)}</span>
        @if status == Failed {
            <p role="alert">This task needs attention.</p>
        }
        {content}
    </section>
}
```

The first parameter is a Roc record. The second is the component body passed by
a paired component tag. There is no implicit `children` property.

## Compose it

```rocci
@component Dashboard = |{}|
    <main>
        <StatusCard title="Nightly build" status={Working}>
            <a href="/builds/latest">View details</a>
        </StatusCard>
    </main>
```

Intrinsic lowercase tags become `Html.element` calls. PascalCase tags call the
corresponding lowered Roc function. Qualified tags such as `<Design.Button />`
call a component imported from another module.

## Add fixture data

```rocci
@fixture{target: StatusCard}
failedCard = {
    title: "Documentation",
    status: Failed,
}
```

Fixtures are normal Roc bindings tagged for previews and tools. `rocci view`
and `rocci browse` use them; the generated value remains available to tests.

## Keep control flow structural

Use `@if`, `@for`, and `@match` when branches produce markup. Use normal Roc
expressions inside text and attributes.

```rocci
@for item in items {
    <StatusCard title={item.title} status={item.status} />
}
```

Markup is not valid inside an arbitrary Roc expression. If a branch returns
markup, make the branch a Rocci directive or call a named rendering function.

## Organize and verify components

Save reusable UI components in a `components/` directory (for example, `components/StatusCard.rocci`).

Inspect and preview the component with `rocci`:

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- inspect --ast components/StatusCard.rocci
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- view components/StatusCard.rocci --component StatusCard
```

To discover, test, and interactively preview every component and fixture in the directory, run the component browser:

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- browse components/
```

Continue with [server actions](/docs/guides/server-actions/) when the component needs to update state.
